Threads : Thread is a light weight smallest part of a program. (a method will be running on one thread)
                                                               (a java project is a process and it has threads)
                                                               (threads are created inside stack)
                                                               (as stack stores temp memory, so thread is temporary)
                                                               (one process runs with help of multiple threads running in it)

- Ways to create a thread :
   Write a class ,
    1. by extending Thread class
    2. implement interface 'Runnable' - Runnable is the best way to create a Thread.

- There will be a 'Thread Scheduler', which creates Thread in stack and gives to us only when there is memory in stack.
- There is no guarantee that Threads will always run one after other. - This is called 'MultiThreading'. MultiThreading runs Threads concurrently.
- Threads usage involves mostly in Banking and Insurance Domains.
- we can sleep a Thread with - Thread.sleep(1000);

1. public class MyThread1 extends Thread {

        // for thread, it only requires a 'run' method which is inside 'Runnable'. so we need to override it.
        // we should mandatorily override the run() method

        @Override
        public void run() {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
            System.out.println("Hi...");
        }

   }

    public class Test {

        // main() itself is also a Thread
        public static void main(String[] args) {

            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());  // to know the name of the current Thread. as main() is also a thread.
            MyThread1 myThread1 = new MyThread1();

            -- not needed this -- myThread1.run();   // but we need to call the start() method which will call run() method, then only the thread will be created.
            // with run() method, the Thread won't gets registered
